# ==============================================================================
#  Template for /srv/healthconnect/secrets.env on webserver.
#
#  NOT `.env.example`, which is what the three sibling stacks call theirs, and the difference is not
#  cosmetic. On those stacks there is one file and the deploy script edits it. Here there are two,
#  and which is which is the whole arrangement:
#
#     .env          GENERATED BY EVERY DEPLOY, overwritten wholesale, kept as .env.previous so a
#                   rollback can restore it. Holds the tag, the channel, the image prefix, the
#                   profile. Nothing long-lived can survive in it — anything added by hand lasts
#                   exactly until the next deploy.
#     secrets.env   CREATED ONCE, BY HAND, and rewritten by no deploy and no rollback. Everything
#                   below. deploy-prod.sh passes it as a second --env-file and never reads a value
#                   out of it, so nothing here can leak into a log, a --dry-run, or `ps`.
#
#  BOTH COMPOSE PROJECTS ON THE HOST READ THIS FILE — the five applications and the five stores — and
#  that is deliberate: the catalog password is written once and two projects use it, rather than
#  twice and drifting. It is the reason the non-secret connection strings live here too. They are
#  not secrets; they are the facts a deploy does not compute, and putting a URL next to the password
#  it authenticates with is what stops the two being changed apart.
#
#  --- HOW TO USE THIS FILE ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  Do not scp it. Create it on the server, from the server, with a restrictive umask, so no value
#  ever exists in a local shell history, a synced dotfile or a chat log:
#
#      ssh webserver
#      mkdir -p /srv/healthconnect && cd /srv/healthconnect
#      umask 077 && cat > secrets.env   # then paste, filled in, and Ctrl-D
#      chmod 600 secrets.env
#
#  GENERATE EVERY SECRET FRESH, ON THE SERVER, with the command given beside it. A value that has
#  been anywhere else is not a secret. Every line below is deliberately EMPTY: this repository is
#  public, and a placeholder that looks real is how a plausible value ends up pasted into production.
#
#  --- WHAT HAPPENS IF ONE IS MISSING ------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  Every key in the REQUIRED section is a `:?` variable in one of the two compose files, so a missing
#  one stops interpolation and nothing starts. deploy-prod.sh's preflight checks all eleven of the
#  application stack's before it touches the running stack, by name, so the failure arrives before
#  .env has been rotated rather than half-way through a roll.
# ==============================================================================

# ==============================================================================
#  REQUIRED — the two estate-wide secrets
# ==============================================================================

# The signing key for every token in this estate.
#
# GENERATE A FRESH ONE. Do NOT take it from ~/webroot/01-healthconnect/.env — that file holds the
# key shared by hc-admin, hc-patient and hc-professional, and hc-market IS NOT IN THAT SET
# (decisions.md D37). Its five services carry their own key, an hc-admin token fails signature
# validation here, and that separation is load-bearing: any service holding a key can mint a token
# for any subject with any authority, so putting the platform key here would silently hand
# hc-market's five services the ability to mint tokens the other three products accept.
#
#   head -c 64 /dev/urandom | base64 -w0
JWT_BASE64_SECRET=

# The erasure pepper — decisions.md D35. Keys the HMAC behind an erased customer's alias, and is the
# only thing standing between a database dump and re-identifying every erased row from a list of
# guessable logins.
#
# ONE VALUE ACROSS BOOKING, CATALOG AND MESSAGING, and it MUST NOT CHANGE once anything has been
# erased: aliases are written into rows in place, nothing re-keys them, and a new pepper leaves
# messaging unable to recognise its own erased subjects. If this host has never been deployed,
# generate one and keep it for ever. If it HAS, the existing value is the only correct one and there
# is no way to recover it from the data — so it is also the one line in this file that must be in
# whatever the organisation uses for key escrow.
#
#   head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64 -w0
HC_PRIVACY_PEPPER=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The FIRST administrator's password — decisions.md D61.
#
# The gateway seeds `admin` on a database that has no administrator, because on an empty production
# database that account is the only way in. It takes the password from here; without it the gateway
# refuses to create the account at all rather than falling back to the value derived from the login,
# which is published in this repository. That refusal fails the health gate and rolls the deploy
# back, so the failure is a deploy that does not land rather than an estate with a known credential.
#
# It is consulted ONLY while no administrator exists. Once one does — and however its password is
# later rotated through the UI — this value is never read again. Leave it here rather than clearing
# it, and do not expect changing it to reset anybody's password.
#
#   head -c 24 /dev/urandom | base64 -w0
HC_GATEWAY_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ==============================================================================
#  REQUIRED — the five stores
#
#  The hostnames below are the container_names in data-compose.yml, resolved over `hcmarketnet`.
#  They are not addresses on any shared network and nothing outside this product can reach them.
#  CI diffs these names against that compose file, because a rename in one and not the other is a
#  datasource URL pointing at a hostname that resolves nowhere — a five-service outage from a
#  one-word edit.
# ==============================================================================

# --- The gateway's MongoDB ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# USE HEX, NOT BASE64, for the password. It is interpolated into a mongodb:// URI, where base64's
# `+`, `/` and `=` are not legal unescaped (RFC 3986): the `/` ends the userinfo section and the
# driver rejects the rest as an invalid host:port, so the gateway fails to start with a message that
# points at the database rather than at the password. hc-professional recorded this the hard way.
# 24 bytes of hex is 192 bits, no weaker than base64 of the same length.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 24
#
# These two are read by the mongo image ONLY when it initialises an EMPTY data directory. Changing
# them later does not change the account — see the README's rotation section before trying.
HC_MONGO_ROOT_USERNAME=hc_market
HC_MONGO_ROOT_PASSWORD=

# SPRING_MONGODB_URI, not SPRING_DATA_MONGODB_URI: Spring Boot 4 renamed the property, and setting
# the old name sets something nothing reads, so application-dev.yml's localhost:27017 wins and the
# gateway dies inside Mongock pointing at a database that is not the problem.
#
# authSource=admin because the credentials above are the container's root user, which lives in the
# admin database and not in the application one.
#
#   HC_GATEWAY_MONGODB_URI=mongodb://<user>:<pass>@hc-market-gateway-db:27017/healthconnectGateway?authSource=admin
HC_GATEWAY_MONGODB_URI=

# --- The four PostgreSQL instances --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One INSTANCE per service, not four schemas in one server, so a service can move hosts without a
# migration and nothing can write a join across two services' data even by accident.
#
# Each password is read TWICE from this file — once by the store that sets it and once by the
# application that authenticates with it — which is exactly why they are in one file and not two.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 24
HC_CATALOG_DB_URL=jdbc:postgresql://hc-market-catalog-db:5432/healthconnectCatalog
HC_CATALOG_DB_PASSWORD=

HC_BOOKING_DB_URL=jdbc:postgresql://hc-market-booking-db:5432/healthconnectBooking
HC_BOOKING_DB_PASSWORD=

HC_MESSAGING_DB_URL=jdbc:postgresql://hc-market-messaging-db:5432/healthconnectMessaging
HC_MESSAGING_DB_PASSWORD=

HC_PAYOUT_DB_URL=jdbc:postgresql://hc-market-payout-db:5432/healthconnectPayout
HC_PAYOUT_DB_PASSWORD=

# ==============================================================================
#  REQUIRED — mail, because an account nobody can activate is not an account
#
#  decisions.md D94, backlog NEW-47. All three are `:?` in docker-compose.prod.yml and defaultless
#  placeholders in application-prod.yml, and deploy-prod.sh checks all three by name before it
#  touches the running stack.
#
#  WHAT THEY REPLACE. This file had no mail section and no compose file passed a single
#  SPRING_MAIL_*, so production would have run on application-prod.yml's committed `localhost:25` —
#  the gateway container's own loopback — with `jhipster.mail.base-url` still set to JHipster's
#  placeholder `http://my-server-url-to-change`. POST /api/register answers 201 without waiting for
#  the mail, MailService catches the failure and logs one WARN line, and the unactivated account is
#  deleted three days later. A customer would have registered, received nothing, been unable to log
#  in, and disappeared — with nothing red anywhere.
# ==============================================================================

# The SMTP relay. NOT localhost: inside the gateway container that is its own loopback, where no
# SMTP server has ever listened, and MailDeliveryGuard refuses it under `prod` for that reason.
#
# The port is required too rather than defaulted, because 25, 465 and 587 mean three different
# things about TLS and authentication and a wrong one fails exactly as silently as a wrong host.
#
#   HC_MAIL_HOST=smtp.your-provider.example
#   HC_MAIL_PORT=587
HC_MAIL_HOST=
HC_MAIL_PORT=

# The origin every activation and password-reset link is composed against — no trailing slash.
#
# THIS IS THE ONE MAIL VALUE THAT FAILS AFTER DELIVERY WORKS. A wrong host here sends a message that
# is accepted by the relay, delivered to the customer, and carries a link to somewhere that does not
# answer; nothing in the estate reports it. Absent, the links are relative and equally useless.
#
# It points at the ORIGIN A PERSON BROWSES, not at the API: the mail composes
# `${baseUrl}/account/activate?key=…`, which is a frontend route. THIS ESTATE HAS NO FRONTEND YET
# (backlog NEW-48), so that link answers 401 today — measured, not 404: reactive Spring Security
# denies an exchange no rule matched, so the person is asked to authenticate to reach the page that
# would let them. The key in it activates the account through `GET /api/activate?key=…`, which
# answers 200 — backlog NEW-60. Until the frontend lands, an operator activating an account for
# somebody does it with the key out of the link.
#
#   HC_MAIL_BASE_URL=https://market.abofonsa.com
HC_MAIL_BASE_URL=

# ==============================================================================
#  OPTIONAL — and each one is optional for a different reason
# ==============================================================================

# --- The relay's credentials, and how it wants to be spoken to — decisions.md D94 ----------------
#
# OPTIONAL, because a relay may need none of it: an MTA on this host's own network authenticates
# nobody, and a provider over the public internet needs both. Blank counts as absent, and neither is
# in deploy-prod.sh's required list — refusing a deploy over a value an estate legitimately does not
# have is how a required variable becomes a placeholder somebody invents.
#
# HC_MAIL_PASSWORD IS A SECRET and belongs in the section above in spirit; it is here because it is
# optional, and it is deliberately not in SECRET_KEYS for the reason just given. It is the only
# value in this file that is somebody else's credential rather than this estate's own.
#
# The two properties default to TRUE in docker-compose.prod.yml — the safe direction, so a relay
# that cannot do STARTTLS fails to send rather than sending this credential in clear. Set them false
# only for a relay you know needs neither, and note the residual: a send that fails is still one WARN
# line, and GET /management/info's `mail.authenticated` is how you see which half is in force.
#
#   HC_MAIL_USERNAME=
#   HC_MAIL_PASSWORD=
#   HC_MAIL_SMTP_AUTH=true
#   HC_MAIL_SMTP_STARTTLS=true

# --- How long an unactivated account is kept — decisions.md D94 -----------------------------------
#
# THREE DAYS BY DEFAULT, and leaving these blank runs exactly what every estate in this project's
# history has run: the figure is JHipster's, it was a literal in a generated file until D94, and the
# decision was to state it rather than change it. It is quoted to a data subject in
# docs/privacy-notice.md 7.1 and to a regulator in docs/processing-record.md 3.1, so CHANGING IT
# HERE MAKES BOTH DOCUMENTS WRONG.
#
# A value that is set and unreadable — or zero, or negative — refuses to start. There is no value
# meaning "never delete"; that is a separate decision (D94 §5).
#
#   HC_UNACTIVATED_ACCOUNT_RETENTION_DAYS=3
#   HC_UNACTIVATED_ACCOUNT_SWEEP_CRON=0 0 1 * * ?

# The Data Protection Commission registration number — decisions.md D42, counsel 2026-09-03.
#
# NOT a secret, and it sits here for a different reason than the values above do: it is a real
# identifier belonging to a real organisation and this repository is public, so it is not ours to
# commit on their behalf. Absent, the stack starts normally, booking logs a warning, and
# GET /api/desk/privacy reports null — which is the honest answer, and is why it is not a `:?`.
# A placeholder here would be a false claim about a real organisation rather than a harmless default.
#
# STILL OUTSTANDING AS OF 2026-09-05: counsel answered "registered" and the number was not supplied.
# The privacy notice and the processing record cannot be published without it (backlog WP-09).
HC_DPC_REGISTRATION=

# Retention periods — decisions.md D42. Counsel's ratified figures are the COMMITTED fallback in
# application.yml, so leaving these blank runs the ratified policy rather than none. Set them here
# only to correct a deployment without cutting a release.
#
# `care-summary-days` is load-bearing beyond configuration: counsel's position that the care summary
# is ordinary contract data rests partly on it being held briefly, so lengthening it is a LEGAL
# change and not a tuning one.
#
#   HC_RETENTION_FINANCIAL_DAYS=2190
#   HC_RETENTION_OPERATIONAL_DAYS=365
#   HC_RETENTION_CARE_SUMMARY_DAYS=90

# --- Payment providers — decisions.md D37, D45 ---------------------------------------------------
#
# ALL THREE ARE OFF, here and everywhere else in this repository, because none of them is
# implemented. The adapters exist, extend ProviderAwaitingIntegration, and fail closed on every call.
#
# TURNING ONE ON MAKES EVERY PRICED BOOKING THAT NAMES IT ANSWER 502. That is the honest behaviour of
# an adapter that has never spoken to its provider, and booking says so at WARN once per enabled
# provider at startup. Do not turn one on to "see if it works".
#
# The secret is what the provider signs its callbacks with. Optional, because a provider nobody
# enabled needs none — and ABSENT MEANS CALLBACKS ARE REFUSED, NOT TRUSTED, which is D35's rule for
# the pepper applied to a provider's key. Blank counts as absent, and an enabled-but-secretless
# provider refuses with the same flat 401 an unimplemented one gives.
#
#   HC_PAYSTACK_ENABLED=false
#   HC_PAYSTACK_SECRET=
#   HC_HUBTEL_ENABLED=false
#   HC_HUBTEL_SECRET=
#   HC_MOMO_ENABLED=false
#   HC_MOMO_SECRET=

# --- Host topology overrides ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The gateway's LOOPBACK-published port. Its default is 8086 in docker-compose.prod.yml and 8086 in
# hc-market-app.conf, written twice because nginx cannot read the environment; CI checks the two
# agree. OVERRIDING IT HERE CHANGES ONLY ONE OF THE TWO and the site returns 502 — change the
# installed nginx snippet in the same maintenance window, or leave it alone.
#
#   HC_GATEWAY_PORT=8086

# The three networks, if this host ever renames one. infranet and monitoring are host-wide and
# belong to ~/webroot/00-infrastructure and ~/webroot/02-monitoring; hcmarketnet is this product's
# and is created by ./infra.sh.
#
#   HC_NETWORK=infranet
#   HC_MONITORING_NETWORK=monitoring
#   HC_DATA_NETWORK=hcmarketnet
